# GLM-5.1-MXFP4 prefill optimization log (gfx950 / DSA path)

Read this before re-profiling or proposing prefill optimizations for GLM5 (or
any DSA / DeepSeek-sparse-attention model) on gfx950. It records what already
shipped and — more importantly — what has already been tried and rejected, so we
do not go in circles.

## Shipped (validated wins)

- **Tier 1 — skip redundant `-inf` indexer fill**: `SGLANG_DSA_SKIP_LOGITS_CLEAN`
  passes `clean_logits=False` on the HIP `fp8_mqa_logits` path (mirrors CUDA).
  ~2-3% median TTFT. (upstream PR sgl-project/sglang#28757)
- **Tier 3 — Triton fp8 sparse-MLA prefill kernel**: `triton_sparse_mla.py`,
  gated in `tilelang_sparse_fwd` behind `SGLANG_DSA_TRITON_PREFILL` (opt-in,
  default off), shape-restricted (`num_heads==16, d_v==512, tail==64`),
  prefill-only. ~1.6x isolated kernel / ~8-12% median TTFT, GSM8K neutral.
  (branch `RM/dsa-triton-prefill`, draft PR sgl-project/sglang#28961)
- **DECODE — EAGLE MTP speculative decoding (biggest decode win, NOT default).**
  `--speculative-algorithm EAGLE --speculative-num-steps 3 --speculative-eagle-topk 1
  --speculative-num-draft-tokens 4`. Works on GLM-5.1-MXFP4 / **MI355X (gfx950)**
  using the checkpoint's MTP/nextn head (no separate draft model). Graphs-on e2e
  (`utilities/e2e_glm5.sh`, 8192/1024): **~2.6-3.3x median ITL** and **+27%
  (conc 64) to +144% (conc 2) output throughput**, holding even at high
  concurrency. Lossless (GSM8K 0.920, invalid 0.000); accept length ~3.4/4.
  This is why it works when compute-kernel swaps didn't: decode is
  collective-bound, and accepting ~3.4 tokens/step cuts forward steps (and thus
  per-token all-reduces) by ~2.7x. **Opt-in, not enabled by default**
  (`utilities/run_glm5.sh --speculative`). NOTE: the GLM-5.1 cookbook currently
  (wrongly) says "EAGLE not supported on AMD" and gates it off in the deploy
  generator — that is stale; EAGLE is validated on MI355X. gfx942 (MI300X/MI325X)
  not yet tested.

## Open prefill levers (current profile, NOT yet tried — promising)

Representative prefill profile (graphs-off=eager=representative; c=16,
num_prompts=64, isl 8192, Triton kernel firing), per-kernel GPU-time share:

| kernel | category | share |
|---|---|---|
| `_sparse_mla_fwd_kernel` (our Triton attn, #28975) | attention | 25.1% |
| `quickreduce allreduce_twoshot` | AllReduce | 20.8% |
| `mfma_moe2_afp4_wfp4` (down, two tiles) | MoE | ~13% |
| `topk_transform_prefill` | indexer | 6.3% |
| `Cijk`/`bf16gemm` proj GEMMs (unquant.py:139) | gemm | ~12% |
| `_gluon_fp8_mqa_logits` | indexer | 5.0% |
| `mfma_moe1_silu_mul` (up/gate) | MoE | 4.2% |
| `add_rmsnorm_quant` | norm | 2.2% |

Two untested levers that do NOT hit the decode graphs-on translation trap
(prefill is eager, so graphs-off tuning/measurement translates directly):

1. **Prefill MoE tuning (different regime from the failed decode tuning).** MoE
   is ~17% of prefill (`mfma_moe2` down ~13% ≫ `mfma_moe1` up 4% — suspicious
   asymmetry, possible bad down-tile at M=8192). The decode MoE tuning regressed
   ONLY because decode runs under CUDA graphs and the tuner's graphs-off pick
   didn't translate. Prefill is eager → a graphs-off `gemm_moe_tune.py` pick for
   the **M=8192 prefill shape** should translate. Validate with an eager e2e TTFT
   A/B (default vs tuned-fmoe), not isolated kernel time.
2. **Prefill AllReduce comm overlap (~21%, REAL not artifact).** For prefill the
   `quickreduce twoshot` is bandwidth-bound real comm on large messages (the
   spin-wait caveat is a DECODE thing). `--enable-two-batch-overlap` (hide comm
   behind compute) or `--enable-aiter-allreduce-fusion` could move prefill TTFT;
   prefill is eager so a straight e2e TTFT A/B is trustworthy.

Attention is #1 at 25% but already the optimized #28975 Triton kernel (~1.6x);
further gains there are MFMA-utilization work, not low-hanging.

## Dead ends — DO NOT re-investigate without new evidence

- **DECODE compute-kernel swaps are a dead end on this config (collective-bound).**
  Three graphs-on e2e A/Bs confirmed compute isn't the decode bottleneck, so
  faster compute kernels don't help (or regress): (a) **split-KV / flash-decode
  sparse-MLA Triton kernel** — ~2-5x in isolated/graphs-off microbench but
  **+4-5% ITL regression** e2e graphs-on (added a split-K scratch round-trip on a
  path graphs already make launch-cheap); (b) **aiter MoE tuning** for the GLM5
  MXFP4 shape (untuned → fallback tile) — ~7-11% faster *isolated kernel* via
  `gemm_moe_tune.py`, but **-1.5% to -4% throughput** e2e because the tuner picks
  by graphs-off microbench, the wrong regime. Lesson: only trust graphs-on e2e
  A/B (`e2e_glm5.sh`) for decode, never isolated kernel time.

- **AllReduce / TP communication.** In **graphs-off** profiles
  (`--disable-cuda-graph`, which is how we profile) the `cross_device_reduce` /
  `quickreduce twoshot` kernels measure **spin-wait idle time** (a rank arrives
  early and busy-waits for the slowest rank), NOT real comm cost. It looks like
  16-21% of prefill but is a profiling artifact in graphs-off traces.
  **Never cite a graphs-off AllReduce % as a lever.** (Correction: rocprof
  segfaults graphs-on, but the torch profiler via `send_one --profile` *does*
  capture graphs-on traces — see Profiling gotchas. Graphs-on, the real decode
  comm is ~7%, not the inflated graphs-off number.) A fused allreduce+residual+
  norm path exists in aiter and is wired behind `--enable-aiter-allreduce-fusion`
  (one server flag, not the kernel-prototyping this entry originally assumed). It
  is **capture-safe on gfx950** and, graphs-on, the fused
  `allreduce_fusion_kernel_1stage` gives ~3% decode ITL / +3-7% throughput at
  **low concurrency (2-8)**. At high concurrency (16-64) it was +0.3-1.2% ITL in
  one run — within the ~1% box noise band; whether that is noise or a small real
  regression is unconfirmed (the fused kernel still fires at high conc — the
  decode AR tensor [batch x hidden] is well under aiter's 64MB cutoff — so it is
  NOT a fallback; the benefit just amortizes away as batch-bound compute grows).
  Net: a low-concurrency-only win, not a dead end — but do NOT expect it to help
  the high-concurrency regime, which is the regime of focus.
- **Tier 2 — sgl-kernel `topk_transform_prefill`**: already an efficient
  two-stage radix partial top-k; no clean win available.
- **`block_I` tuning of the TileLang sparse kernel**: 64 is optimal. 128 = ~-19%
  prefill (occupancy loss), 32 fails to compile (MFMA warp partition). Knob
  exhausted.
- **Two-pass softmax rewrite of the TileLang sparse kernel**: -18% prefill. The
  second QK+KV-load pass costs more than the per-iteration `acc_o` rescale it
  removes; low MFMA% is dependency-stall, not spare throughput.
- **`aiter` prefill backend (`--dsa-prefill-backend aiter`)**: incompatible with
  the fp8 KV-cache layout (`kv.view(-1,1,1,576)` fails; our store is
  divisible-by-512, not 576). Would need a dequant/layout shim (bf16, slower).
  `flashmla_*` / `trtllm` / `fa3` are CUDA-only (import fail on ROCm). On gfx950,
  **TileLang is the only working fp8 DSA attention backend** besides our Triton
  kernel.

## Profiling gotchas (gfx950)

- **Graphs-on profiling IS possible via `send_one --profile` (corrects an earlier
  wrong claim).** rocprof and `bench_one_batch --enable-profile-cuda-graph`
  crash/segfault on graphs-on trace export in this image (ROCTracer
  `corrupted size vs prev_size` heap abort). But the standard
  `generate-profile` skill path — a normally-launched server (CUDA graphs ON) +
  `python3 -m sglang.test.send_one --profile --profile-by-stage` — exports clean
  per-stage `-DECODE` / `-EXTEND` traces. Graphs-off traces overstate launch
  bubbles + AllReduce spin-wait; for any launch/occupancy/comm conclusion use the
  graphs-on `send_one` trace, not a graphs-off one.
- **Profile prefill with a REAL workload, not a toy request.** A degenerate
  capture (`bench_serving --num-prompts 2 --max-concurrency 1 --profile-num-steps 1`)
  produced a prefill EXTEND trace where our Triton kernel showed **0 launches**
  and TileLang `main_kernel` ran instead — the toy request never hit the
  `q_nope.shape[0] >= 512` gate, so it was a capture artifact, NOT a regression
  (the merged #28975 is byte-identical to the validated code). Profile prefill
  via the `e2e_glm5.sh` per-point workload (e.g. c=16, num_prompts=64, isl 8192)
  with `--profile --profile-by-stage`; under that, `_sparse_mla_fwd_kernel` fires
  (312 launches, 25% of prefill) as expected. Prefill runs **eager** (only decode
  is graph-captured), so a graphs-off prefill trace is representative.
- **`bench_one_batch` monolithic prefill crashes on current upstream**: a single
  un-chunked extend > ~16384 tokens overflows the new `eager_runner` /
  `cuda_graph_buffer_registry` buffer (`_foreach_copy_`: 16384 vs 131072). This
  is upstream, not our code, and fails with our kernels off too. For profiling
  use `batch*input <= 16384` (kernel fractions are token-count-invariant), or the
  server + `bench_serving` (chunked prefill stays within the buffer).
- **Triton autotune pollutes rocprof kernel-trace**: the first-call autotune
  benchmarks ~18 configs, inflating `_sparse_mla_fwd_kernel` call count
  (e.g. 746 vs ~78/forward) and its GPU-time share to a bogus ~56%. Time-windowing
  does not separate it (all forwards bunch at trace end). For a clean per-kernel
  prefill breakdown use the **torch profiler on one post-warmup step**
  (`--profile --profile-stage prefill --profile-steps 1`) + `glm5_prof_csv.py`,
  not rocprof whole-trace.

## Decode optimization methodology (graphs-on discipline) — READ BEFORE TOUCHING DECODE KERNELS

The core trap: decode runs under HIP graphs (graphs-on), so you **profile in one
regime (graphs-on, in-context) but optimize in another (graphs-off, in-isolation),
and graphs-off's optimum is not graphs-on's optimum — it is often anti-correlated.**
A kernel made faster standalone (bigger tiles, more work/block) raises register/LDS
pressure, drops occupancy, and destroys the inter-kernel overlap that was hiding it
graphs-on → faster alone, slower in the graph. Decode kernels are
overlap/occupancy-bound, not throughput-bound. Also: a kernel reported at Y ms in the
graphs-on trace is **not** its marginal cost (part of Y overlaps a neighbor), so
halving it may not move step time at all. (See the `block_I=64` and two-pass softmax
dead ends — both are this trap.)

The discipline that actually holds for decode here:

- **The only verdict is graphs-on end-to-end step time (or ITL).** The kernel trace
  is a *hypothesis generator*, never a decision. Rank suspects from the graphs-on
  `-DECODE` breakdown, but accept/reject changes only on the graphs-on step delta.
- **A/B the whole graph, never the kernel.** Recapture the graph with the variant and
  compare graphs-on decode step time side-by-side (`run_glm5_ab.sh`). The unit of
  optimization is the recaptured graph, not the standalone kernel.
- **If you must microbench, reproduce the adjacency**: real decode shapes (batch=C,
  seqlen=1, real head/expert dims), warm L2 with the actual producer, and ideally
  bench a *captured subgraph* of the kernel + its neighbors — NOT a bare
  `for _ in range(N): kernel(same_buffers)` loop, which measures the one regime that
  never occurs in production.
- **Prefer optimization classes that survive graphs-on**: fusion / boundary removal
  (kills a real dependency edge + a memory round-trip — helps *more* graphs-on),
  reducing memory traffic, and reducing register/LDS pressure to *raise*
  occupancy/overlap. "Tune tile/unroll for standalone GEMM throughput" is the class
  that evaporates or inverts graphs-on. This is why every shipped win is a fusion and
  every isolated-kernel-throughput attempt was rejected.
- **Never compare a graphs-off microbench number to a graphs-on trace number.** They
  are different physical regimes.

## Hardware / config facts

- Only **TP4** is sensible for GLM5-MXFP4 on 270GB gfx950: weights are 396GB, so
  TP1 is impossible (>1 GPU of weights), TP2 is impractical (~198GB/GPU leaves
  ~16GB KV at mem-fraction 0.85), TP8 is wasteful. TP4 → **16 heads/rank** (why
  the Triton kernel is shape-gated to `num_heads==16`).
- gfx950 uses fp8 **e4m3fn** (not fnuz); gfx942 uses e4m3fnuz. Use
  `is_fp8_fnuz()`, not `torch.version.hip`.
